{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1263579-an-untraditional-host-mountain-pine-beetle-host","id":1263579,"title":"An untraditional Host: Mountain Pine Beetle Host","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-10-30T08:10:29-07:00","id":1263579,"title":"An untraditional Host: Mountain Pine Beetle Host","content":"One of the fundamental assumptions of Hosts thus far is that they use humans as vessels. I thought it'd be fun to subvert that assumption with a Host that colonizes trees, rather than humans.<br /> I was inspired by learning about the ongoing epidemic of Mountain Pine Beetles that is currently devastating forests across the Western United States up into portions of Canada. They're pretty unassuming little guys, about the size of a rice grain, with plain black bodies. Their life cycle starts with an adult female beetle, which seeks out a stressed or unhealthy pine tree and bores under its bark to lay its eggs. The tree will attempt to defend itself by producing copious quantities of resin to &quot;pitch out&quot; the beetle, but unhealthy trees or those being swarmed en masse will not be able to produce enough pitch to prevent the infestation. The beetle is also adapted to carry a specific fungus which blocks the tree's ability to transport water, and therefore produce pitch.<br /> If she's successful, she releases a pheromone that draws other beetles to join the attack. Once under the bark, she excavates galleries where she will lay her eggs. The larvae hatch several days later, and begin boring their way through the phloem of the tree. The phloem is basically the vascular system of the tree, so in combination with the effects of the blue stain fungus, as the larvae chew through the phloem they cut off the flow of nutrients from the roots to the rest of the tree, resulting in its death. After chewing around under the bark for almost a full year, they emerge as adults and continue the cycle. In this whole process, the adult beetle spends only a couple of days in the open air.<br /> <br /> I think the body horror of this process is pretty evident, but if you don't believe me, watch <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wR5O48zsbnc\" target=\"_blank\">this</a>. I recommend turning off the sound and putting on your favorite grimdark music. <br /> <br /> Historically, pine beetle infestations are a pretty normal and ecologically healthy cycle. In the last few decades, several factors (drought and fire suppression, mostly) have conspired to create a massive epidemic of pine beetles that are absolutely devastating forest across North America.<br /> <br /> <br /> I think all of this makes for great fodder for a Host. I'm imagining some kind of mythology related to a servitor of a powerful Fire-spirit, devoted to the creation of fuel for its master. It works relentlessly to engineer an apocalyptic conflagration that will burn Flesh and Spirit to ashes, and make room for the rebirth of a new world where its burning god is supreme.<br /> The part I'm a bit stuck on is what it actually looks and acts like as an antagonist. I can imagine something super cheesy with trees that become animated by the Hosts, but I'm looking for something subtler and scarier. The idea here is for a Host that the uratha are even less-prepared to deal with than the Azlu or Beshilu- one that doesn't show its presence in any obvious way, and doesn't rely on humans for its metamorphosis (and so can better hide in the wilderness). <br /> <br /> Thought be welcome.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/22980-robothedino","name":"robothedino","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}